1 TITL Capitán
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} El capitán Pedro Mexía de Lugo nació entre 1540 y 1543. Era sobrino-nieto del gobernador, justicia mayor y regidor perpétuo de Tenerife Don Bartolomé Benítez Pereira de Lugo y primo segundo del gobernador de Puerto
2 CONC  Rico Francisco Bahamonde de Lugo. El capitán don Pedro llegó a Puerto Rico el 20 de agosto de 1565 desde las Islas Canarias, junto a su primo (a quién lo llamaba su "tío") Francisco Bahamonde de Lugo. Proviene de los muy nobles l
2 CONC inajes de la familia directa del conquistador y Adelantado de Canarias Alonso Fernández de Lugo y de los “Mexía de Figueroa” (descendiente directo de los Maestres de la Orden de Santiago Gonzalo Mexía y Alonso Suárez de Figueroa).
2 CONC  Ciertamente, fue una de las figuras más destacadas de su tiempo en Puerto Rico.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aparece como esposo de doña Isabel de Sotomayor y padre del capitán Juan de Lugo Sotomayor en el libro:
2 CONT
2 CONT FERNÁNDEZ DE BÉTHENCOURT, Francisco. Nobiliario y blasón de Canarias. Diccionario histórico, biográfico, genealógico y heráldico de la provincia. Tomo I. Santa Cruz de Tenerife: Imprenta Isleña y Encuadernación de Libros de Franci
2 CONC sco C. Hernández, Castillo, 51, 1878, 59.
2 CONT
2 CONT También en el testamento de su suegro, Hernán Pérez de Valenzuela de 1581, AGI, Contaduría 1075, folios 662v-667v. 
2 CONT
2 CONT El capitán don Pedro Mexía de Lugo es el eslabón que une los datos genéticos entre las familias Sotomayor y Colón de Puerto Rico y el linaje Pereira del Norte de Portugal. Para más ver este artículo en la revista ''Journal of Gene
2 CONC alogy and Family History'': https://www.qualifiedgenealogists.org/ojs/index.php/JGFH/issue/view/3
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Pedro Mexía de Lugo was born between 1540 and 1543. He was the great-nephew of the governor, justice major and perpetual alderman of Tenerife Don Bartolomé Benítez Pereira de Lugo and second cousin of the governor of Puert
2 CONC o Rico Francisco Bahamonde de Lugo. Captain Don Pedro arrived in Puerto Rico on August 20, 1565 from the Canary Islands, along with his cousin (whom he called his "uncle") Francisco Bahamonde de Lugo. It comes from the very noble 
2 CONC lineages of the direct family of the conqueror and Adelantado of the Canary Islands Alonso Fernández de Lugo and the "Mexía de Figueroa" (direct descendant of the Masters of the Order of Santiago Gonzalo Mexía and Alonso Suárez de
2 CONC  Figueroa). Certainly, he was one of the most outstanding figures of his time in Puerto Rico.
2 CONT
2 CONT He appears as the husband of Doña Isabel de Sotomayor and father of Captain Juan de Lugo Sotomayor in the book:
2 CONT
2 CONT FERNÁNDEZ DE BÉTHENCOURT, Francisco. Nobility and coat of arms of the Canary Islands. Historical, biographical, genealogical and heraldic dictionary of the province. Tomo I. Santa Cruz de Tenerife: Imprenta Isleña y Encuadernación
2 CONC  de Libros de Francisco C. Hernández, Castillo, 51, 1878, 59.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also in the will of his father-in-law, Hernán Pérez de Valenzuela of 1581, AGI, Accounting 1075, folios 662v-667v.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Don Pedro Mexía de Lugo is the link that unites the genetic data between the Sotomayor and Columbus families of Puerto Rico and the Pereira lineage of Northern Portugal. For more see this article in the Journal of Genealog
2 CONC y and Family History:
2 CONT
2 CONT Posted to my Geni public Profile Comments Thread.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Prof. Antonio de Sotomayor, Ph.D. December 24, 2015 at 6:49 PM
2 CONT
2 CONT Hello Theresa. Ana de Lugo Sotomayor (wife of Diego Montañez de Sierra) was the daughter of Captain Don Juan de Lugo Sotomayor (bc 1590, San Juan) and Doña Luisa Velásquez (bc. 1592, San Juan). Don Juan de Lugo Sotomayor was the s
2 CONC on of Don Pedro Mexia de Lugo (bc. 1565, San Juan) and Doña Isabel de Sotomayor y Pérez del Rincón (bc. 1570, San Juan). The possible conection here would be Doá Isabel de Sotomayor, but she inherited her surname Sotomayor from an
2 CONC  ancestor (possible a grandmother or great grandmother) because her parents were Don Herán Pérez de Valenzuela and Doña María del Rincón. Sometimes daughters would inherit surnames from female ancestors as an act of respect and ho
2 CONC nor. Don Hernán's parents were Don Alonso de Lepe and Doña Catalina Perez. Don Hernán and his parents were from Almonte, Andalucía, Spain. The Sotomayors are one family and descend from Men Paes de Sorred who was granted the Lords
2 CONC hip over the valley of Souto in Pontenvedra, Galicia in the 1100s by King Alfonso VI and named it Souto Maior (Great Grove). All the Sotomayors descend from this man, but many have inherited the lastname through female lines, like
2 CONC  the case of Isable illustrate. I hope this helps. Merry Christmas!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is the profile, he was looking at, too, when he formulated this. One of my 6th great grandfathers. ~Theresa Renée Delgado-Tossas<br/>
