2 CONT In a discussion in the FB group of the New Mexico Genealogical Society, an experienced genealogist claims that there is no evidence to support this relationship (see below)
2 CONT
2 CONT José Antonio Esquibel- When Diego de Vera was tried by the Inquisition in 1630, he named each of his siblings (AGN, Inquisition, vol. 495). He had one brother named Pedro de Vera and three sisters. No Gaspar. Also, the certified c
2 CONC opy of the marriage record of Diego de Vera and doña María de Abendaño records the names of his parents as Pedro de la Vera Perdomo and María de Betanzos. In a testimony that he provided in February 1626 he named his parents as Pe
2 CONC dro Perdomo and doña María de Betanzos (AGN, Inq., vol. 495). Three years later, in July 1629, he named his parents as Pedro de Vera Perdomo and doña María de Betanzos (AGN, Inq., vol 495). The three records that give his mother's
2 CONC  surname as Betanzos are based on information provided directly by Diego de Vera. There is one record in which his mother's name is written as Betancor, but this may have been an error on the part of the scribe/notary. It was that
2 CONC  particular record that Fray Angélico Chavez extracted that provided the Betancur surname in his book, "Origins of New Mexico Families."
