2 CONT Posted to my Geni public Profile Comments Thread.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Prof. Antonio de Sotomayor, Ph.D. December 24, 2015 at 6:49 PM
2 CONT
2 CONT Hello Theresa. Ana de Lugo Sotomayor (wife of Diego Montañez de Sierra) was the daughter of Captain Don Juan de Lugo Sotomayor (bc 1590, San Juan) and Doña Luisa Velásquez (bc. 1592, San Juan). Don Juan de Lugo Sotomayor was the s
2 CONC on of Don Pedro Mexia de Lugo (bc. 1565, San Juan) and Doña Isabel de Sotomayor y Pérez del Rincón (bc. 1570, San Juan). The possible conection here would be Doá Isabel de Sotomayor, but she inherited her surname Sotomayor from an
2 CONC  ancestor (possible a grandmother or great grandmother) because her parents were Don Herán Pérez de Valenzuela and Doña María del Rincón. Sometimes daughters would inherit surnames from female ancestors as an act of respect and ho
2 CONC nor. Don Hernán's parents were Don Alonso de Lepe and Doña Catalina Perez. Don Hernán and his parents were from Almonte, Andalucía, Spain. The Sotomayors are one family and descend from Men Paes de Sorred who was granted the Lords
2 CONC hip over the valley of Souto in Pontenvedra, Galicia in the 1100s by King Alfonso VI and named it Souto Maior (Great Grove). All the Sotomayors descend from this man, but many have inherited the lastname through female lines, like
2 CONC  the case of Isable illustrate. I hope this helps. Merry Christmas!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is the profile, he was looking at, too, when he formulated this. One of my 6th great grandfathers. ~Theresa Renée Delgado-Tossas<br/>
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/people/Laurencio-Ram%C3%ADrez-de-Arellano-de-Lugo/6000000004192871944
2 CONT ----
2 CONT http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/h/e/r/Jocelyn-Hernandez/WEBSITE-0001/UHP-0577.html
2 CONT
2 CONT View Tree for Antonio Ramírez de Arellano Figueroa Antonio Ramírez de Arellano Figueroa (b. 04 Mar 1653, d. date unknown)
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonio Ramírez de Arellano Figueroa (son of Lázaro Ramírez de Arellano Benavides and Clara de Figueroa Vargas)241 was born 04 Mar 1653, and died date unknown. He married Ana Lugo y Sotomayor in San German, Puerto Ríco.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Includes NotesNotes for Antonio Ramírez de Arellano Figueroa: Book: Familias de Cabo Rojo
2 CONT
2 CONT -Enrique Ramírez Brau tried to establish who was the father of Remigio.
2 CONT
2 CONT -According to Mr. Acosta, there is no documented proof, but agrees with Mr. Brau that Remigio was the son of the well known "Corregidor de San Germán" Antonio Ramírez de Arellano.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Mr. Brau found some documentation with testimonies of several neighbors of San Germán,  regarding the parents and grandparents of Antonio as; Lázaro Ramírez de Arellano,  already dead in 1693- the date of the testimony, and Clara
2 CONC  de Figueroa.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Ms. Acosta also states that on a personal conversation with Lluch Mora, he mentioned that Antonio Ramíres de Arellano (Capitán y Teniente a Guerra) reched to the rank of Brigadier General.
2 CONT
2 CONT -In that conversation, she states they discussed that Ana Lugo, Antonio´s wife,  was also from the Buenaventura & Sotomayor.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Antonio was also defender of the Island from the governor of the Island of Tortuga,  Monsieur Beltran D'Oregon.
2 CONT
2 CONT -D'Oregon invaded the island in Piñales, near the Añasco River in 1673.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Antonio was born on the 4th of March, 1653 & won the battle against D'Oregon in Piñales when he was only 20 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Ursula Acosta & David E. Cuesta Camacho, Familias de Cabo Rojo, (1st Edition, 1983). Pg 91
2 CONT
2 CONT May 21, 2006 I found this article on my notes from Dr. Ursula Acosta´s notes published on her website.
2 CONT
2 CONT LA LIMPIEZA DE SANGRE OF MARIA NICOMEDA RAMIREZ DE ARELLANO -Ursula Acosta 
2 CONT
2 CONT Researchers of the Ramírez de Arellano family have taken it for granted that two of the most prominent members of this family, Antonio Ramírez de Arellano (born 1653) and Remigio Ramírez de Arellano (died 1766) were father and son
2 CONC . 
2 CONT
2 CONT However. until recently there was no proof that this assumption was true. It was only several years ago, when the General Archives of Puerto Rico organized the documents of the Superior Court of Mayagüez (Tribunal Superior de Maya
2 CONC güez), that I found in these old court records a file evaluating the racial purity of a couple to be married in 1828 in San German (Limpieza de Sangre) as was customary for persons of higher social standing. This rather long and d
2 CONC eteriorated document establishes the relationship between Antonio and Remigio Ramírez de Arellano without the shadow of a doubt. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As many of the members of this Society are related to the Ramírez de Arellano family, I thought that it might be of interest to publish the information of this particular Limpieza although I can only refer to my notes. It should, 
2 CONC of course, be possible to check the original documents in the files of the General Archives, but when I wanted to read them again several years ago, I could not find them. As they were not in a good condition to begin with, it is 
2 CONC possible that they are no longer available. However, I am sorry to say that it has been my experience that some visitors to the Archives - I do not believe that these persons deserve the title of "researcher" - either disappear do
2 CONC cuments or hide them in some file with the purpose of limiting access to them. For that reason I think it possible that this particular Limpieza has not been necessarily lost to termites and age, but may be in the hands of an arch
2 CONC ival scavenger.  The bride who had to show that she was white and a descendant of a family of good social standing in San Germán was doña María Nicomeda Ramírez de Arellano y Ramírez de Arellano. She was born in San Germán on Sept
2 CONC ember 15, 1800, and baptized there on the 28th 
2 CONT
2 CONT of the same month. In the following I will give her ascendency as stated in the Limpieza and some other information the witnesses provide and which may be of interest to researchers of the Ramírez de Arellanos.  María Nicomedas pa
2 CONC rents were don Joaquín (Calixto) Ramírez de Arellano and doña María Concepción Ramírez de Arellano who were third cousins (third degree of consanguinity) and had married in San Germán on February 8, 1792. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her grandparents were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a. Don Lázaro Ramírez de Arellano married to doña Sebastiana Quiñones; and 
2 CONT
2 CONT b. Don Manuel Ramírez de Arellano married to doña Valentina de Rivera. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The four sets of her greatgrandparents. beginning with Don Lázaros parents, were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a. Don Baltazar Ramírez de Arellano married to doña Juana de la Seda: 
2 CONT
2 CONT b. Don Juan Isidro Quiñones married to doña Micaela Mirabal; 
2 CONT
2 CONT c. Don Remigio Ramírez de Arellano married to doña Ana de Matos (sic); and 
2 CONT
2 CONT d. Don Eusebio de Rivera married to doña Polonia Vélez Borrero. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The greatgrandparents of don Joaquín Calixto Ramírez de Arellano, the father of the bride, were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a. Don Antonio Ramírez de Arellano married to doña Ana Martínez de Matos (sic): 
2 CONT
2 CONT b. Don Bartolomé de la Seda married to doña Ursula (illegible) de Bonilla: 
2 CONT
2 CONT c. Don Andrés Quiñones married to doña Ana Montalvo: and 
2 CONT
2 CONT d. Don Sebastián Mirabal married to doña María Segarra y Verdugo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The reader may notice that the conclusion is obvious. If María Nicomedas parents were third cousins, her grandparents Lázaro and Manuel Ramírez de Arellano must havc been first cousins, and, in turn, their fathers, Baltazar and Re
2 CONC migo RamIrez de Arellano, brothers. This means that Antonio Ramírez de Arellano was the father of both, Baltazar and Remigio. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *The document was prepared in 1828 spanning information over a period of almost 150 years of family history. It is only natural that some names of the older generations were misquoted. It is well known that Don Remigios first wife
2 CONC  was Maria de Ia Cruz Martínez de Matos, and not "Ana de Matos". 
2 CONT *A further mistake is the name of Don Antonios wife. She was Ana de Lugo y Sotomayor. These understandable mistakes do not, in my opinion. invalidate the conclusion. It may be added that it is interesting to note that no name of t
2 CONC he men of thge family was incorrect. Although the Catholic Church required its parishioners to inform the names of the female members of a family because of the prohibition and/or restriction of cousin marriages, they still did no
2 CONC t appear to be quite as important as the names of the male members and seem to have been forgotten more easily. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was customary that many witnesses testified to the facts of a case. For that reason the Limpieza offers the investigator more information than the relationships given above. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Both. don Sebastián Mirabal and don Manuel Ramírez de Arellano. had at one time been alférez real of San Germán. Don Manuel Estevan de Rivera. one of the witnesses of the Lirnpieza, said that the paternal granduncle of the bride,
2 CONC  don José Monserrate Ramírez de Arellano, was once alcalde ordinario of the same town. He also knew Don Juan. Don Nicolás and Don Felipe Ramírez de Arellano. sons of Don Remigio. Don Juan had been regidor perpetuo in San Germán. w
2 CONC hile Don Nicolás served both as alcalde ordinario in San Germán on many occasions and teniente a guerra in Cabo Rojo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He also knew don José Ramón Ramírez, a first cousin of Doña Nicomeda and son of her uncle Don Francisco Antonio. Both, father and son, had been the alcalde ordinario of San Germán on two separate occasions. José Ramón was, in 1828
2 CONC , subdelegado militar de la marina, a position also held on another occasion by his father. According to the samc witness an uncle of the bride, don José Monserrate RamIrez. was mayor of San Gerrnán at the time this Limpieza was w
2 CONC ritten. This José Monserrate Ramírrez was the son of the former alcalde of the same name.  Another nephew of Joaquin Calixto Ramírez was don Vicente Ramírez, a captain of the 4th Battalion of the Militia, while one of Don Joaquíns
2 CONC  sons, Don Ramón, was a sublieutenant in the same battalion. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The next witness. Captain of the Cavalry don Vicente Dávila, was from Añasco and knew the mayor of Sabana Grande. don Tomás José RamIrez, as well as don Tomás Ramírez, mayor and teniente a guerra of Cabo Rojo. He also refers to an
2 CONC other uncle of the bride, don Francisco Antonio Ramírez. father of the Don José Ramón who was mentioned above. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Another witness from Añasco was don José Vicente González, married and a farmer (labrador). 
2 CONT
2 CONT (If it surprises us that some of the witnesses came from Añasco. I remind the reader that one of Remigios sons, Felipe. had moved to Añasco where he had been a prominent member of the ruling class with ties to San Germán and Añasc
2 CONC o. ) 
2 CONT
2 CONT An interesting witness was don Faustino del Toro, interesting because at the time of the deposition he was 77 years old and had known many of the members of the older generation, among them don Juan Isidro Quiñones, doña Micaela M
2 CONC irabal, don Manuel Rarmírez and the latters wife, doña Valentina de Rivera, as well as her mother, doña Polonia Vélez Borrero. He confirms that Don Nicolás was the founder of Cabo Rojo, and that Don Nicolass first cousin, Don Juan
2 CONC , had been mayor in Mayagüez where he was followed in office by don José Ramírez. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He finally stated a surprising fact. According to Don Faustino, the founder and first teniente a guerra of Sabana Grande had been don Dámaso Ramírez, whom he had known personally. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In my book Familias de Cabo Rojo (1983, Tablas. pp. 65 and 68) we find a Dámaso Ramírez, son of Laurencio and Antonia Martínez de Matos. Laurencio. who was a brother of Remigio Ramírez de Arellano, died in 1786 in Sabana Grande. 1
2 CONC  had written "de Cabo Rojo" as it appeared to me that the Sabana Graude where Laurencio died referred to the area between San Germán and Cabo Rojo, which is a continuation of the barrio Sabana Eneas. However, the Limpieza seems to
2 CONC  indicate that I was wrong and that Laurencio and his son Dámaso were the founders or, at least, among the founders of the town of Sabana Grande. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A final witness was don Miguel Mauricio Quiñones, 63 years old, a farmer and brother of Juan Isidro Quiñones. He reiterates the information about the founders of Sabana Grande calling Don Dámaso the primary founder. 
2 CONT
2 CONT My own investigation shows that Manuel Ramirez de Arellano, maternal grandfather of María Nicomeda, also was the father of a well-known figure of early 19th-century Cabo Rojo, don Marcos Ramírez de Arellano (Acosta, 1983, Tables, 
2 CONC p. 70). Marcos Rarnírez had his farm at the Morrillos in Cabo Rojo and is an ancestor of the family of the late Severo Colberg (through his son Justo RamIrez), of my husband Sebastián Acosta Ronda (through his son Nicolás) and of 
2 CONC many other caborrojeños. Both Manuel Ramírez and his wife Valentina de Rivera y Vélez Borrero had died before 1792 when their daughter Maria Concepción married Don Joaquín Ramírez de Arellano. 
2 CONT
2 CONT BIBLIOGRAPHY 
2 CONT
2 CONT Acosta, U. and Cuesta, D. (1983) Familias de Cabo Rojo. San Juan, PR.  Archivo General de Puerto Rico. Fondo del Tribunal Superior de Mayagüez. 
2 CONT
2 CONT See also http://www.genealogy.com/ftm/h/e/r/Jocelyn-Hernandez/WEBSITE-0001/UHP-0577.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonio Ramírez de Arellano Figueroa (son of Lázaro Ramírez de Arellano Benavides and Clara de Figueroa Vargas)241 was born 04 Mar 1653, and died date unknown.He married Ana Lugo y Sotomayor in San German, Puerto Ríco.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Includes NotesNotes for Antonio Ramírez de Arellano Figueroa: Book: Familias de Cabo Rojo
2 CONT
2 CONT -Enrique Ramírez Brau tried to establish who was the father of Remigio.
2 CONT
2 CONT -According to Mr. Acosta, there is no documented proof, but agrees with Mr. Brau that Remigio was the son of the well known "Corregidor de San Germán" Antonio Ramírez de Arellano.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Mr. Brau found some documentation with testimonies of several neighbors of San Germán,  regarding the parents and grandparents of Antonio as; Lázaro Ramírez de Arellano,  already dead in 1693- the date of the testimony, and Clara
2 CONC  de Figueroa.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Ms. Acosta also states that on a personal conversation with Lluch Mora, he mentioned that Antonio Ramíres de Arellano (Capitán y Teniente a Guerra) reched to the rank of Brigadier General.
2 CONT
2 CONT -In that conversation, she states they discussed that Ana Lugo, Antonio´s wife,  was also from the Buenaventura & Sotomayor.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Antonio was also defender of the Island from the governor of the Island of Tortuga,  Monsieur Beltran D'Oregon.
2 CONT
2 CONT -D'Oregon invaded the island in Piñales, near the Añasco River in 1673.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Antonio was born on the 4th of March, 1653 & won the battle againstD'Oregon in Piñales when he was only 20 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:Ursula Acosta & David E. Cuesta Camacho, Familias de Cabo Rojo,(1st Edition, 1983). Pg 91 --Could be that some of the children are repeats.
2 CONT
2 CONT It seems there is a mention of them in the book, but then on Pg.92 Ms. Acosta states that Mr. Brau has stated Antonio had 10 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT May 21, 2006 I found this article on my notes from Dr. Ursula Acosta´s notes published on her website.
2 CONT
2 CONT LA LIMPIEZA DE SANGRE OF MARIA NICOMEDA RAMIREZ DE ARELLANO -Ursula Acosta  Researchers of the Ramírez de Arellano family have taken it for granted that two of the most prominent members of this family, Antonio Ramírez de Arellano
2 CONC  (born 1653) and Remigio Ramírez de Arellano (died 1766) were father and son. However. until recently there was no proof that this assumption was true. It was only several years ago, when the General Archives of Puerto Rico organi
2 CONC zed the documents of the Superior Court of Mayagüez (Tribunal Superior de Mayagüez), that I found in these old court records a file evaluating the racial purity of a couple to be married in 1828 in San German (Limpieza de Sangre) 
2 CONC as was customary for persons of higher social standing. This rather long and deteriorated document establishes the relationship between Antonio and Remigio Ramírez de Arellano without the shadow of a doubt. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [As many of the members of this Society are related to the Ramírez de Arellano family, I thought that it might be of interest to publish the information of this particular Limpieza although I can only refer to my notes. It should,
2 CONC  of course, be possible to check the original documents in the files of the General Archives, but when I wanted to read them again several years ago, I could not find them. As they were not in a good condition to begin with, it is
2 CONC  possible that they are no longer available. However, I am sorry to say that it has been my experience that some visitors to the Archives - I do not believe that these persons deserve the title of "researcher" - either disappear d
2 CONC ocuments or hide them in some file with the purpose of limiting access to them. For that reason I think it possible that this particular Limpieza has not been necessarily lost to termites and age, but may be in the hands of an arc
2 CONC hival scavenger. ]
2 CONT
2 CONT The bride who had to show that she was white and a descendant of a family of good social standing in San Germán was doña María Nicomeda Ramírez de Arellano y Ramírez de Arellano. She was born in San Germán on September 15, 1800, a
2 CONC nd baptized there on the 28th of the same month. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following I will give her ascendency as stated in the Limpieza and some other information the witnesses provide and which may be of interest to researchers of the Ramírez de Arellanos. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''María Nicomedas parents were :" don Joaquín (Calixto) Ramírez de Arellano and doña María Concepción Ramírez de Arellano who were third cousins (third degree of consanguinity) and had married in San Germán on February 8, 1792. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Her grandparents were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a. Don Lázaro Ramírez de Arellano married to doña Sebastiana Quiñones; 
2 CONT
2 CONT and 
2 CONT
2 CONT b. Don Manuel Ramírez de Arellano married to doña Valentina de Rivera. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *The four sets of her greatgrandparents. beginning with Don Lázaros parents, were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a. Don Baltazar Ramírez de Arellano married to doña Juana de la Seda: 
2 CONT
2 CONT b. Don Juan Isidro Quiñones married to doña Micaela Mirabal; 
2 CONT
2 CONT c. Don Remigio Ramírez de Arellano married to doña Ana de Matos (sic); 
2 CONT
2 CONT and 
2 CONT
2 CONT d. Don Eusebio de Rivera married to doña Polonia Vélez Borrero. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *The greatgrandparents of don Joaquín Calixto Ramírez de Arellano, the father of the bride, were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a. Don Antonio Ramírez de Arellano married to doña Ana Martínez de Matos (sic): 
2 CONT
2 CONT b. Don Bartolomé de la Seda married to doña Ursula (illegible) de Bonilla: 
2 CONT
2 CONT c. Don Andrés Quiñones married to doña Ana Montalvo: 
2 CONT
2 CONT and 
2 CONT
2 CONT d. Don Sebastián Mirabal married to doña María Segarra y Verdugo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The reader may notice that the conclusion is obvious. If María Nicomedas parents were third cousins, her grandparents Lázaro and Manuel Ramírez de Arellano must havc been first cousins, and, in turn, their fathers, Baltazar and Re
2 CONC migo RamIrez de Arellano, brothers. This means that Antonio Ramírez de Arellano was the father of both, Baltazar and Remigio. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The document was prepared in 1828 spanning information over a period of almost 150 years of family history. It is only natural that some names of the older generations were misquoted. It is well known that Don Remigios first wife 
2 CONC was Maria de Ia Cruz Martínez de Matos, and not "Ana de Matos". A further mistake is the name of Don Antonios wife. She was Ana de Lugo y Sotomayor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT These understandable mistakes do not, in my opinion. invalidate the conclusion. It may be added that it is interesting to note that no name of the men of thge family was incorrect.  Although the Catholic Church required its parish
2 CONC ioners to inform the names of the female members of a family because of the prohibition and/or restriction of cousin marriages, they still did not appear to be quite as important as the names of the male members and seem to have b
2 CONC een forgotten more easily. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was customary that many witnesses testified to the facts of a case. For that reason the Limpieza offers the investigator more information than the relationships given above. Both. don Sebastián Mirabal and don Manuel Ramírez de
2 CONC  Arellano had, at one time, been alférez real of San Germán. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Manuel Estevan de Rivera. one of the witnesses of the Lirnpieza, said that the paternal granduncle of the bride, don José Monserrate Ramírez de Arellano, was once alcalde ordinario of the same town. He also knew Don Juan. Don 
2 CONC Nicolás and Don Felipe Ramírez de Arellano. sons of Don Remigio. Don Juan had been regidor perpetuo in San Germán. while Don Nicolás served both as alcalde ordinario in San Germán on many occasions and teniente a guerra in Cabo Ro
2 CONC jo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He also knew don José Ramón Ramírez, a first cousin of Doña Nicomeda and son of her uncle Don Francisco Antonio. Both, father and son, had been the alcalde ordinario of San Germán on two separate occasions. José Ramón was, in 1828
2 CONC , subdelegado militar de la marina, a position also held on another occasion by his father. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the samc witness an uncle of the bride, don José Monserrate RamIrez. was mayor of San Gerrnán at the time this Limpieza was written. This José Monserrate Ramírrez was the son of the former alcalde of the same name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Another nephew of Joaquin Calixto Ramírez was don Vicente Ramírez, a captain of the 4th Battalion of the Militia, while one of Don Joaquíns sons, Don Ramón, was a sublieutenant in the same battalion. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The next witness. Captain of the Cavalry don Vicente Dávila, was from Añasco and knew the mayor of Sabana Grande. don Tomás José RamIrez, as well as don Tomás Ramírez, mayor and teniente a guerra of Cabo Rojo. He also refers to an
2 CONC other uncle of the bride, don Francisco Antonio Ramírez. father of the Don José Ramón who was mentioned above. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Another witness from Añasco was don José Vicente González, married and a farmer (labrador). If it surprises us that some of the witnesses came from Añasco. I remind the reader that one of Remigios sons, Felipe. had moved to Añasco
2 CONC  where he had been a prominent member of the ruling class with ties to San Germán and Añasco. 
2 CONT
2 CONT An interesting witness was don Faustino del Toro, interesting because at the time of the deposition he was 77 years old and had known many of the members of the older generation, among them don Juan Isidro Quiñones, doña Micaela M
2 CONC irabal, don Manuel Rarmírez and the latters wife, doña Valentina de Rivera, as well as her mother, doña Polonia Vélez Borrero. He confirms that Don Nicolás was the founder of Cabo Rojo, and that Don Nicolass first cousin, Don Juan
2 CONC , had been mayor in Mayagüez where he was followed in office by don José Ramírez.  He finally stated a surprising fact. According to Don Faustino, the founder and first teniente a guerra of Sabana Grande had been don Dámaso Ramíre
2 CONC z, whom he had known personally. In my book Familias de Cabo Rojo (1983, Tablas. pp. 65 and 68) we find a Dámaso Ramírez, son of Laurencio and Antonia Martínez de Matos. Laurencio. who was a brother of Remigio Ramírez de Arellano,
2 CONC  died in 1786 in Sabana Grande. 1 had written "de Cabo Rojo" as it appeared to me that the Sabana Grande where Laurencio died referred to the area between San Germán and Cabo Rojo, which is a continuation of the barrio Sabana Enea
2 CONC s. However, the Limpieza seems to indicate that I was wrong and that Laurencio and his son Dámaso were the founders or, at least, among the founders of the town of Sabana Grande. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A final witness was don Miguel Mauricio Quiñones, 63 years old, a farmer and brother of Juan Isidro Quiñones. He reiterates the information about the founders of Sabana Grande calling Don Dámaso the primary founder.  My own invest
2 CONC igation shows that Manuel Ramirez de Arellano, maternal grandfather of María Nicomeda, also was the father of a well-known figure of early 19th-century Cabo Rojo, don Marcos Ramírez de Arellano (Acosta, 1983, Tables, p. 70). Marco
2 CONC s Rarnírez had his farm at the Morrillos in Cabo Rojo and is an ancestor of the family of the late Severo Colberg (through his son Justo RamIrez), of my husband Sebastián Acosta Ronda (through his son Nicolás) and of many other ca
2 CONC borrojeños. Both Manuel Ramírez and his wife Valentina de Rivera y Vélez Borrero had died before 1792 when their daughter Maria Concepción married Don Joaquín Ramírez de Arellano. 
2 CONT
2 CONT BIBLIOGRAPHY  Acosta, U. and Cuesta, D. (1983) Familias de Cabo Rojo. San Juan, PR.  Archivo General de Puerto Rico. Fondo del Tribunal Superior de Mayagüez.  More About Antonio Ramírez de Arellano Figueroa and Ana Lugo y Sotomayo
2 CONC r: Marriage: San German, Puerto Ríco.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Includes NotesMarriage Notes for Antonio Ramírez de Arellano Figueroa and Ana Lugo y Sotomayor: Book: Familias de Cabo Rojo
2 CONT
2 CONT -Dr. Acosta states that Brau listed these as the children of Antonio & Ana.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eugenio- Married to María del Rosario martínez de Matos
2 CONT
2 CONT Germán- Married to Teresa Pabón Dávila
2 CONT
2 CONT Esteban- María del Toro y Quiñones
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan- Married to Ana Martínez de Matos y Espinosa
2 CONT
2 CONT Sebastián- Married to Juana de Mirabal
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro- Married to Margarita Benavides
2 CONT
2 CONT Petronilla- Married to José Ramos Colón
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrés- Married to Juana de Rivera
2 CONT
2 CONT Juana- Married to Baltazar Sepúlveda
2 CONT
2 CONT and possibly
2 CONT
2 CONT Laurencio- Married to Antonia Martínez de Matos
2 CONT
2 CONT -Dr. Acosta makes a point that Petronila cannot be the sister of Remigiuo, but a cousin. In the marriage of Valentín Ramírez de Arellano with María Belén Ramírez de Arellano in 1819 in Cabo Rojo, she concludes that the Great-Grand
2 CONC mother of Valentín, Petronila, & the Great- Grandfather of Marís Belén, Eugenio...were brothers.It indicates that Petronila was first cousin to the son of Eugenio (2º with 3º grados de Consanguinidad)- Petronila being the niece of
2 CONC  Eugenio.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Another evidence she found that proved Petronila is not a sister, but a niece to Remigio, is that ifPetronila is a niece of both Remigio and Eugenio- they they must be brothers.She believes there may have been a mistake in the re
2 CONC dording of the Consanguinity in the marriage of 1819, having to do with the Great Grandparents of the bride and groom.
2 CONT
2 CONT -She concludes that Antonio has the following children: RemigioJuanEugenioEstebanJuanaLaurencio and possibly these... -Baltazar-Sebastián-Pedro-Andrés
2 CONT
2 CONT Also that Germán was a first cousin, and Petronila is the niece of Remigio & Eugenio & not a sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:Ursula Acosta & David E. Cuesta Camacho, Familias de Cabo Rojo,(1st Edition, 1983). Pg 92-93
2 CONT
