0 @N6000000005024488298@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT He had 2 wives at sametime,one in New M exico and the other in the Canary Islands. He was ordered to leave New Mexico and return to the Canary Islands.  
1 CONT _____________________________
1 CONT Diego de Vera came to Santa Fé sometime before 1622, and on 16 Jan of that year he married María de Abendaño, daughter of Simón de Abendaño and María Ortíz (Baca), both deceased. The witnesses were Don Pedro Durán y Cháves and his
1 CONC  wife, Isabel de Bohórquez (Baca), aunt of the bride. Three years later, Fray Alonso Benavides came to Santa Fé as head of the Church in New Mexico, but also representing the Inquisition; as a layman he had been its sheriff in the
1 CONC  Canary Islands. Now, Diego de Vera had a wife in Tenerife in the Canaries. The presence of Father Benavides finally compelled him to go to the Padre and discolse his bigamous status. when he left New Mexico in 1626, Father Benavi
1 CONC des took him along to México CVity, and there Diego was tried by the Holy Officel. But becuase Benavides pleaded that he had confessed the crime voluntarily, and had been a good encomendero in New Mexico, personally teaching the c
1 CONC atechism to the Indians under him, Diego got off with an easy sentence from the Holy Office. He was to return the Canaries and never come back to New Mexico. He sailed for Europe in the company of good Father Benavides.
1 CONT Diego was thirty-three in 1626 when he revealed his bigamy. His Santa Fé wife was mentioned as a grand-daughter of Capitán Juan López Holguín. They had two children, both girls. These were María, who became the wife of Manuel Jorg
1 CONC e, and then of Diego de Montoya; and Petromila, who married Pedro Romero. After the ammulment, their mother married Antonio de Salas.
1 CONT Since Diego de Vera, through his two daughters, became the ancestor of leading New Mexicans in later generations, it is well to give his own genealogy, which out during his trial. His parents were Pedro de Vera Perdomo and María d
1 CONC e Betancur, residents of the City of La Laguna on Tenerife. His paternal grandparents were Hernán Martín Baena, a native of Jerez de los Caballeros in Estremadura, Spain, and Catalina García, native of La Laguna on Tenerife. His m
1 CONC aternal grandparents were Antonio Pérez, born on the Canary Island of La Graciosa, and Catalina Aponte, native of Garachico on Tenerife.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1626 Diego de Vera (ONMF: 112) named his parents as "Pedro de bera perdomo y Da Ma de bentanco," and declared he was a native of the "isla de tenerife". This information comes from a record that Fray Angélico Chávez consulted f
1 CONC or his source on Diego de Vera in ONMF. Curiously, Chávez made no mention of the following note written by fray Alonso de Benavides: "Este diego de Vera es sobrino de la perdoma que por esse Sto tribunal fue castigada aura veinte 
1 CONC y tres años con una hija suja y dises que unos dos hermanas o hermanos deste se avian ido aberb_cia y ay mal sospecha". This information describes Diego de Vera as a nephew (sobrino) of a woman referred to as 'la Perdoma' who was 
1 CONC punished by the Inquisition about twenty-three years earlier (circa 1603) along with her daughter and, as he heard said, two sisters or two brothers. It is not clear what the behavior was regarded as "mala sospecho" (bad suspicion
1 CONC ). Two possibilities is that 'la Perdoma' was tried and sentenced for practicing witchcraft or for practicing Jewish ceremonies. Whatever the circumstances, it appears that 'la Perdoma' was a sister of Diego's father, Pedro de Ver
1 CONC a Perdomo. 
1 CONT
1 CONT It would be very worthwhile to locate the Inquisition records of 'la Perdoma' as they would very likely contain additional genealogical information on the Vera Perdomo family. In additions, the records could provide some interesti
1 CONC ng history about members of the Vera Perdomo family. If she was tried and sentenced as a "judiazante" (a baptized Catholic practicing Jewish religious customs), this could reveal a Jewish-converso lineage with a connection to New 
1 CONC Mexico.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is not clear if 'la Perdoma' was tried by the Inquisition in Nueva España or on the island of Tenerife, but there was another person in New Mexico who had information about this circumstance. On 27 January 1626, Matías López de
1 CONC  Castillo (ONMF: 55), age 35, provided testimony in which he declared that Diego de Vera "era sobrino de una muger que avia aestado prese en la inquisision" ("is nephew of a woman that was imprisioned through the Inquisition"). 
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Archivo General de la Nación, México, Inquisisión, t. 356, f. 306 & 307.
1 CONT
1 CONT __________________________________________________________________
1 CONT The Inquisition trial records of Diego de Vera [ONMF: 112] provide additional family information than the names of his parents and grandparents as given by Fray Angélico Chávez. On trial at México City in January 1626 for bigamy, 
1 CONC Diego de Vera declared he was a native of La Laguna on the island of Tenerife (Canary Islands), and gave his age as 33 (b.ca. 1592-93). In addition to naming his parents and grandparents (see ONMF: 112), he named a paternal uncle,
1 CONC  Martín Baena of La Laguna. He also named another uncle whose given name he could not recall but who was surnamed Perdomo and was married in Burgos and had a daughter Juana Perdomo who was married with Juan Bautista de Lanso (?) [
1 CONC the text appears to read Lanso], and this couple had three children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Diego de Vera also made reference to another uncle named Cristóbal Martín of Garachico (Canary Islands) and another relative named Ynés Martín, a native of Garachico, who was married to a man named Blas Reyes. Blas Reyes and Ynés 
1 CONC Martín were the parents of two children, María and Cristóbal. Deigo de Vera also mentions the names of two siblings, Juana Perdomo and María Perdomo, the latter married with Antonio González.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his disposition, Diego de Vera named his first wife as Catalina Pérez, a native of the pueblo of La Vega [Bega in the text], Isla de Tenerife [Canary Islands], and a daughter of Salvador González [Gonsáles in the text] and Marí
1 CONC a Magdalena. Diego and Catalina were married at the pueblo de la Vega and one of the witnesses to this union was Juana de Perdoma, Diego's sister.  This couple resided in La Vega for about a year before relocating to the pueblo of
1 CONC  Telde where they lived for about two years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Diego de Vera and Catalina Pérez had two children, a son named Pedro and another son whose name is not given. After about three years of marriage, Diego apparently left his first wife and came to New Spain. He traveled on the ship
1 CONC  of Don Juan de la Cueva, and spent around three to four years in New Spain, living for a time in the city of Puebla de los Ángeles with a first cousin named Juana Perdomo. He eventually became mayordomo of the wagon trains going 
1 CONC to New Mexico where he married his second wife Doña María de Abendaño in January 1622.
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: John B. Colligan
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Archivo General de la Nación, México, Inquisición, tomo 356, f. 267, 270, 303, 306 and tomo 495, fo. 90-102
1 CONT ____________________________________
